WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:07.880
We will now move from analyzing ideal
spectra to analyzing real spectra.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:10.770
This is one of the T-Rex spectra.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:16.250
And as soon as we come up with an idea
of how the peptides that generated this

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:23.250
spectrum look like, we can immediately
start annotating peaks in the spectrum.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:27.983
For example,
this peak has a mass explained by

00:00:27.983 --> 00:00:32.948
a prefix peptide of length 10,
while this peak

00:00:32.948 --> 00:00:37.925
has a mass explained by a
suffix peptide of length 3.

00:00:37.925 --> 00:00:43.683
In total, we were able to annotate
six peaks in the spectrum

00:00:43.683 --> 00:00:50.827
using this conjecture about the peptides
that generated this spectrum, and

00:00:50.827 --> 00:00:57.531
we say that the "Shared Peak Count" for
this spectrum and peptide is 6.

00:00:57.531 --> 00:01:04.380
This is another idea about the peptides
that generated the same spectrum.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:07.800
And in this case,
the shared peak count is higher:

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:13.156
we are able to annotate 10,
rather than 6 peaks in the spectrum.

00:01:13.156 --> 00:01:17.860
Which peptide, the first one or

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:23.850
the second one, did Asara propose as
the peptide generating DinosaurSpectrum?

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:28.430
You may be surprised with this answer
because it may look like the peptide

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:33.290
at the bottom is definitely
a better candidate for

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:34.760
explaining the DinosaurSpectrum.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.050
There's two alternatives.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:44.900
Bring up the question of how should
we score an annotated spectrum?

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:48.308
Should we score it as a shared peak count?

00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:54.780
Or maybe we should simply sum up
the intensities of all explained peaks.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:58.910
Both options have some disadvantages.
For example,

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.879
shared peak count ignores intensity.

00:02:01.879 --> 00:02:05.005
Sum of intensity, on the other hand,

00:02:05.005 --> 00:02:09.802
may lead to problems when large
peaks dominate the score.

00:02:09.802 --> 00:02:15.283
Our goal is to come up with
a probabilistic model of spectra so

00:02:15.283 --> 00:02:21.741
that large peaks contribute to the score
but do not dominate the score.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:27.663
To address this challenge,
mass spectrometrists

00:02:27.663 --> 00:02:32.019
came up with this concept
of a "Spectral Vector,"

00:02:32.019 --> 00:02:40.760
a transformation of a spectrum of mass m into an
m-dimensional vector: &lt;s1, ..., si, ..., sm&gt;.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:44.593
The value si, which is known as amplitude,

00:02:44.593 --> 00:02:49.463
approximates the likelihood
that mass i is the prefix of

00:02:49.463 --> 00:02:53.832
an unknown peptide that
generated the spectrum.

00:02:53.832 --> 00:02:58.700
You may be wondering how we can
transform a spectrum into a spectral

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:03.848
vector if we don't know the peptides
that generated this spectrum,

00:03:03.848 --> 00:03:10.553
but this is a subject of a detour that
I will not cover in this lecture.

00:03:10.553 --> 00:03:13.833
To elaborate on the concept
of spectral vectors,

00:03:13.833 --> 00:03:17.840
we will first introduce a simple
notion of "Peptide Vector."

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:25.380
Given a peptide, we will take each amino
acid in this peptide of mass m and

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:30.650
represent it as an m-dimensional
binary vector with

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:36.290
m-1 0s in the beginning,
and and single 1 in the end.

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:42.547
Afterwards, we will concatenate
all vectors corresponding

00:03:42.547 --> 00:03:48.329
to individual amino acids into
a peptide vector shown in this slide.

00:03:48.329 --> 00:03:54.032
You can easily solve the following
problem: Given a peptide, convert it

00:03:54.032 --> 00:03:59.437
into a peptide vector, and it is clear
that we can also solve a reverse

00:03:59.437 --> 00:04:04.664
problem: Given a peptide vector,
transform it into a peptide.

00:04:04.664 --> 00:04:09.246
We will now turn to a more
difficult problem of converting

00:04:09.246 --> 00:04:12.920
a spectrum into a spectral vector.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:17.900
Let's start by analyzing this peak and
converting it into an amplitude.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:21.120
In this case it turns out
the amplitude for this peak is +9.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:28.230
Know that it is important that +9
is not the intensity of this peak.

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:32.720
It is the likelihood that this
peak will be annotated by a

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:38.290
prefix of an unknown peptide
that generated this spectrum.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:42.585
Let's press it for this peak,
it's a very short one, hardly visible.

00:04:42.585 --> 00:04:47.258
The amplitude will be -5, and
amplitude may be negative or positive.

00:04:47.258 --> 00:04:51.580
For this peak, it is +7 and
for this peak, it is +3.

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:55.472
We will combine them all into
a single spectral vector,

00:04:55.472 --> 00:04:59.633
which is an integer-valued
vector with m coordinates.

00:04:59.633 --> 00:05:03.261
And typically,
the larger the peak at mass i,

00:05:03.261 --> 00:05:07.913
the larger the value of amplitude
of the spectral vector, but

00:05:07.913 --> 00:05:13.328
the dependencies between intensities and
amplitudes are complex.

